## Control hierarchy

1. Tenant and management groups define organizational scope.
2. Subscription placement establishes platform and workload boundaries.
3. Azure Policy evaluates location, tagging, diagnostics, encryption, network,
   and service-specific controls.
4. Repository policy checks reject IaC changes that weaken the baseline.
5. Protected environments authorize deployment.
6. Continuous compliance and drift checks detect out-of-band changes.

#{{IF governance.policyBaseline.enforcementMode == 'audit'}}
> Policy is configured in audit mode. Findings are visible but not blocked.
> Promotion to deny requires a clean compliance report, approved exemptions,
> and a tested rollback path.
#{{ELSE}}
Policy enforcement is blocking. Any exemption must be explicit, scoped,
time-bound, owned, and reviewed before expiry.
#{{ENDIF}}

## Policy lifecycle

| State | Required evidence |
|---|---|
| Propose | Business requirement, affected scopes, test cases |
| Audit | Compliance impact and false-positive review |
| Remediate | Owner and due date for existing resources |
| Enforce | Clean pilot, exception record, rollback plan |
| Retire | Replacement control or accepted risk |

## Exception standard

Every exception must record:

- control and resource scope;
- business justification and compensating controls;
- accountable owner and approver;
- creation and expiration dates;
- remediation plan;
- evidence link.

Permanent undocumented exceptions are not permitted.

